  "title": "Subset Sum Exists",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Knapsack",
  "patterns": [
    "knapsack",
    "subset sum",
    "dynamic programming"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "each number is either included in the subset or not - what would a table of 'is this sum reachable' look like, built up one number at a time?",
    "define dp[s] as true if some subset of the numbers processed so far sums to exactly s, with dp[0] = true always (the empty subset)",
    "for each number num, a sum s becomes reachable either because it already was, or because s - num was reachable before adding num: dp[s] = dp[s] OR dp[s - num]",
    "update s from target down to num for each number, so you don't let the same number contribute twice to the same sum in one pass",
    "after processing every number, return dp[target] directly"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "iterating the sum update from low to high, which allows the same number to be reused multiple times toward the same subset",
    "forgetting dp[0] = true, which breaks every derivation that relies on 'reachable sum plus this number'",
    "not handling target = 0 as trivially true before or within the DP"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of non-negative integers and a target value.\n\n**Determine whether some subset of the list, possibly empty, possibly all of it, sums to exactly the target, and return true if such a subset exists, false otherwise.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= nums.length <= 200",
      "0 <= nums[i] <= 1000",
      "0 <= target <= 10^4"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "target is 0 (always true via the empty subset)",
      "target exceeds the sum of every element (always false)",
      "a single element equal to the target"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
